1
00:00:29,375 --> 00:00:31,416
<i>At 12:25 a.m.,</i>

2
00:00:31,500 --> 00:00:34,166
<i>Otis made landfall on the Acapulco coast</i>
<i>as a Category 5 hurricane.</i>

3
00:00:34,250 --> 00:00:35,583
<i>In less than 12 hours,</i>

4
00:00:35,666 --> 00:00:39,166
<i>Otis went from a tropical storm</i>
<i>to a Category 5 hurricane.</i>

5
00:00:39,250 --> 00:00:41,333
BASED ON THE LIVES OF MARLON VALDEZ
AND JONATHAN TRINIDAD

6
00:00:41,416 --> 00:00:43,708
<i>The Acapulco we knew is gone.</i>

7
00:00:43,791 --> 00:00:46,625
<i>A paradisiacal landscape has been</i>
<i>replaced by these images of devastation.</i>

8
00:00:46,708 --> 00:00:48,791
<i>Acapulco is totally destroyed.</i>

9
00:00:48,875 --> 00:00:50,250
Neighborhoods of Acapulco

10
00:00:50,333 --> 00:00:52,291
<i>have been affected by the hurricane.</i>

11
00:00:52,375 --> 00:00:54,583
<i>Thousands of hotels, houses,</i>
<i>and businesses were destroyed.</i>

12
00:00:54,666 --> 00:00:57,875
<i>Otis, now the strongest storm ever</i>
<i>to hit Mexico.</i>

13
00:00:57,958 --> 00:01:01,000
<i>No one was prepared</i>
<i>for such a brutal change.</i>

14
00:01:01,083 --> 00:01:04,208
<i>The population was not evacuated,</i>
<i>the warnings came too late.</i>

15
00:01:04,291 --> 00:01:07,500
<i>They continued</i>
<i>searching for their relatives,</i>

16
00:01:07,583 --> 00:01:11,333
<i>most of whom were</i>
<i>working on boats last October 24.</i>

17
00:01:33,291 --> 00:01:40,291
SURVIVING HURRICANE OTIS

18
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:56,833
NATIONAL WEATHER SERVICE

19
00:02:00,208 --> 00:02:03,041
48 HOURS BEFORE THE HURRICANE

20
00:02:27,250 --> 00:02:29,000
<i>-Hello.</i>
-We have a problem.

21
00:02:37,666 --> 00:02:40,041
May I introduce you to <i>El Majestuoso.</i>

22
00:02:44,625 --> 00:02:46,625
They call <i>him </i>"El Majestuoso"?

23
00:02:46,708 --> 00:02:49,666
No! No, this is <i>El Majestuoso.</i>

24
00:02:53,083 --> 00:02:56,916
And this handsome guy here
is Wirpul, our sailor.

25
00:02:57,458 --> 00:03:00,833
If you need anything,
he's at your service. Right, Wirpul?

26
00:03:00,916 --> 00:03:02,041
Sure.

27
00:03:02,125 --> 00:03:03,416
Who wants a piña colada?

28
00:03:03,500 --> 00:03:04,333
-Me.
-Me.

29
00:03:04,416 --> 00:03:05,458
-Me.
-Let's go.

30
00:03:05,541 --> 00:03:07,708
-I thought it was smaller.
-But it looks great.

31
00:03:07,791 --> 00:03:10,166
Be very careful boarding.
Wirpul will help you.

32
00:03:10,250 --> 00:03:12,250
-This way, please.
-Thanks, thanks.

33
00:03:12,333 --> 00:03:13,458
It really is incredible.

34
00:03:13,541 --> 00:03:16,250
INSPIRED BY TRUE EVENTS

35
00:03:16,333 --> 00:03:17,916
ACAPULCO, MEXICO

36
00:03:28,458 --> 00:03:30,791
You always have to make the same joke.

37
00:03:31,541 --> 00:03:32,666
It always works.

38
00:03:34,666 --> 00:03:36,875
Pour them extra strong
so they get drunk fast.

39
00:03:36,958 --> 00:03:38,125
I want to leave early.

40
00:03:39,416 --> 00:03:41,125
Where'd you steal those bottles from?

41
00:03:41,208 --> 00:03:43,125
They're not stolen if someone left them.

42
00:03:43,958 --> 00:03:46,000
It's the same as stealing, isn't it?

43
00:03:46,708 --> 00:03:47,958
What's your problem?

44
00:03:48,500 --> 00:03:51,625
Do you think it's honest
to sell liquor that isn't yours?

45
00:03:51,708 --> 00:03:55,000
The thing is, you have a different idea
of what honesty is.

46
00:03:55,083 --> 00:03:56,750
We get by with what we have.

47
00:03:57,250 --> 00:04:00,583
Or where do you think these juniors
get the money to come on vacation?

48
00:04:06,500 --> 00:04:08,208
I think I clogged the toilet.

49
00:04:12,125 --> 00:04:14,541
This is what being a sailor is all about.

50
00:04:14,625 --> 00:04:16,750
Well, I don't want to be a sailor.

51
00:04:16,833 --> 00:04:19,208
Oh, really? Then what do you want to be?

52
00:04:20,750 --> 00:04:21,625
Well, a captain.

53
00:04:22,333 --> 00:04:23,166
A captain.

54
00:04:24,583 --> 00:04:26,125
Well, guess what, asshole?

55
00:04:26,208 --> 00:04:29,916
God heard you, and starting tomorrow
you're going to be captain.

56
00:04:32,250 --> 00:04:34,166
You got promoted, you bastard.

57
00:04:35,458 --> 00:04:36,916
-Are you serious?
-Yeah!

58
00:04:37,791 --> 00:04:39,833
How? Who promoted me?

59
00:04:39,916 --> 00:04:43,250
Me, of course. I'm too old
to keep putting up with this mess.

60
00:04:43,333 --> 00:04:46,500
Besides, it's already been discussed
with the boss and everything.

61
00:04:46,583 --> 00:04:47,708
Me? The captain?

62
00:04:48,625 --> 00:04:50,333
-Me?
-Yeah, you.

63
00:04:50,833 --> 00:04:52,625
That's what you wanted, right?

64
00:04:52,708 --> 00:04:54,791
Where there's a will,
there's a way, bastard.

65
00:04:54,875 --> 00:04:58,416
But anyway, remember that it doesn't
take effect until tomorrow, captain.

66
00:04:58,500 --> 00:04:59,833
Clean that up today.

67
00:05:15,500 --> 00:05:18,375
Mochis, you've got to be
more convincing, huh?

68
00:05:19,333 --> 00:05:22,333
Come on, look like
you haven't eaten in three days.

69
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:25,333
That's it!

70
00:05:25,416 --> 00:05:26,250
Let's go!

71
00:05:27,833 --> 00:05:28,833
Let's go, Mochis.

72
00:05:30,333 --> 00:05:32,958
Go find a customer,
and I'll catch up with you in a minute.

73
00:05:33,041 --> 00:05:33,958
That's it!

74
00:05:38,291 --> 00:05:39,500
Hey!

75
00:05:39,583 --> 00:05:42,750
Oh, honey, look!
He's missing a little paw.

76
00:05:42,833 --> 00:05:44,750
Oh, you damn dog! There you are!

77
00:05:44,833 --> 00:05:46,791
I've been looking for you!
Come here, don't bother the girl!

78
00:05:46,875 --> 00:05:48,458
No! No, he's not bothering me!

79
00:05:48,541 --> 00:05:50,625
Hey, your dog's really cute, huh?

80
00:05:50,708 --> 00:05:51,958
But he looks tired.

81
00:05:52,041 --> 00:05:56,250
It's just that we've been walking all day
and business has been really slow.

82
00:05:56,333 --> 00:05:57,666
And what do you sell?

83
00:06:00,125 --> 00:06:02,916
Dogfish empanadas. They're really tasty.

84
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:04,791
How much for the whole basket?

85
00:06:15,958 --> 00:06:17,583
This is a robbery, asshole!

86
00:06:22,583 --> 00:06:25,291
I'm gonna fuck you up if you don't!

87
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:28,083
I'm gonna fuck you up, you bastard!

88
00:06:32,166 --> 00:06:35,500
I don't mess around, you bastard!
I'm gonna fuck you up, you bastard!

89
00:06:36,791 --> 00:06:39,666
No way, fucking Cándido!

90
00:06:39,750 --> 00:06:43,166
I'd recognize you
even if you had a full body mascot suit.

91
00:06:44,375 --> 00:06:46,250
What trouble are you in now?

92
00:06:46,333 --> 00:06:47,416
What do you mean, "what trouble"?

93
00:06:47,500 --> 00:06:49,708
I haven't been able
to find a job at any shop.

94
00:06:50,291 --> 00:06:53,208
Couldn't you help me out
with some cash, Mr. Rafa?

95
00:06:54,416 --> 00:06:55,708
I'll sell you my gun.

96
00:06:56,333 --> 00:06:57,208
It's a Glock.

97
00:06:58,708 --> 00:07:00,500
Don't fuck with me, Cándido!

98
00:07:01,041 --> 00:07:02,666
That's not a Glock!

99
00:07:02,750 --> 00:07:05,166
-What the fuck do you mean it's not?
-You know nothing about guns.

100
00:07:05,250 --> 00:07:06,333
Of course I know about guns.

101
00:07:08,125 --> 00:07:09,000
Let's see.

102
00:07:11,375 --> 00:07:13,666
It's all clean. My dad left it for me.

103
00:07:14,333 --> 00:07:18,625
If it was your dad's, it's probably
already got two or three bodies on it.

104
00:07:18,708 --> 00:07:20,041
No way! What are you talking about?

105
00:07:20,125 --> 00:07:25,708
Don't take this the wrong way,
but Chevo was a real son of a bitch.

106
00:07:29,416 --> 00:07:30,958
Eight hundred pesos.

107
00:07:32,083 --> 00:07:33,166
What the fu--?

108
00:07:33,750 --> 00:07:34,583
What?

109
00:07:34,666 --> 00:07:37,083
Two thousand five hundred
for old times' sake, right?

110
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:42,291
Nine hundred, and I won't tell your mom
you tried to rob me.

111
00:07:44,708 --> 00:07:46,791
Two thousand, and I won't tell my mom
who bought the gun from me.

112
00:07:46,875 --> 00:07:52,208
One thousand, and I won't tell your mom
you're selling guns.

113
00:07:56,000 --> 00:07:57,000
All right, deal.

114
00:08:00,666 --> 00:08:06,000
Why can't you be more like your brother
and get yourself a decent job?

115
00:08:07,666 --> 00:08:09,583
You're a really lousy thief.

116
00:08:10,583 --> 00:08:11,625
Well, yeah.

117
00:08:13,625 --> 00:08:15,416
A lousy thief, but an excellent salesman.

118
00:08:15,500 --> 00:08:19,458
I came to sell you the gun.
I was going to let you have it for 500.

119
00:08:20,625 --> 00:08:22,291
Oh, by the way.

120
00:08:23,041 --> 00:08:26,000
That other idiot is my half-brother,
that's why he's kind of…

121
00:08:26,625 --> 00:08:27,708
confused.

122
00:08:28,208 --> 00:08:29,666
See you later, Mr. Rafa!

123
00:08:29,750 --> 00:08:30,750
Asshole.

124
00:08:31,833 --> 00:08:34,625
<i>The National Weather Service</i>
<i>is issuing a warning</i>

125
00:08:34,708 --> 00:08:38,625
<i>and urging extreme caution</i>
<i>ahead of Otis's arrival in Guerrero.</i>

126
00:08:38,708 --> 00:08:40,291
<i>It's forecasted to make landfall</i>
<i>as a tropical storm</i>

127
00:08:40,375 --> 00:08:41,208
<i>the day after tomorrow,</i>

128
00:08:41,291 --> 00:08:42,875
<i>bringing heavy rain, intense gusts of wind</i>

129
00:08:42,958 --> 00:08:44,625
<i>and possible high waves in coastal areas…</i>

130
00:08:44,708 --> 00:08:47,208
Did you already know that?
The bay's gonna be closed.

131
00:08:47,291 --> 00:08:49,833
Yeah, but for tourists, not for sailors.

132
00:08:50,500 --> 00:08:53,125
Tomorrow you'll take the helm
watching over the boat.

133
00:08:54,791 --> 00:08:57,333
Can't you wait until tomorrow?

134
00:08:57,416 --> 00:08:58,541
No, you see,

135
00:08:58,625 --> 00:09:02,083
tomorrow I'll be putting out towels
on the lounge chairs at the Princess.

136
00:09:02,666 --> 00:09:04,500
You got a job at the Princess?

137
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:07,791
Look, Wirpul,
I'm going to be straight with you.

138
00:09:07,875 --> 00:09:11,291
No one else would give a captain's job
to someone your age.

139
00:09:11,375 --> 00:09:13,833
Especially coming
from the family you come from.

140
00:09:17,083 --> 00:09:18,750
Find yourself a sailor for tomorrow,

141
00:09:18,833 --> 00:09:21,375
bring some beers,
some tacos, and that's it.

142
00:09:21,458 --> 00:09:24,125
You're going to have a great time,
you'll see.

143
00:09:25,833 --> 00:09:30,083
Hey, by the way, how are you guys
managing without your dad?

144
00:09:33,375 --> 00:09:34,458
Well.

145
00:09:36,666 --> 00:09:37,500
Hey!

146
00:09:38,125 --> 00:09:39,083
Captain!

147
00:09:40,625 --> 00:09:41,666
Captain Wirpul!

148
00:09:42,375 --> 00:09:44,083
Captain, you bastard!

149
00:09:45,375 --> 00:09:46,291
Captain Wirpul!

150
00:09:47,041 --> 00:09:49,250
Captain Wirpul.

151
00:10:14,500 --> 00:10:15,708
POLANCO, MEXICO CITY

152
00:10:15,791 --> 00:10:17,166
Damn, that was delicious, man.

153
00:10:17,250 --> 00:10:18,125
Delicious.

154
00:10:23,666 --> 00:10:26,291
This is bullshit,
Mary didn't clean a thing.

155
00:10:26,375 --> 00:10:28,666
Relax, man. She'll come back soon.

156
00:10:29,750 --> 00:10:31,250
Pour us some wine, huh?

157
00:10:31,750 --> 00:10:32,708
Sure.

158
00:10:43,666 --> 00:10:44,833
What's up, Sasha?

159
00:10:45,541 --> 00:10:47,291
We're waiting for you, baby.

160
00:10:47,375 --> 00:10:50,666
Where are you guys?
This is going to go on until dawn.

161
00:10:50,750 --> 00:10:52,916
<i>Don't rule out that Otis</i>
<i>could go from a tropical storm</i>

162
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:54,750
<i>to a Category 5 hurricane…</i>

163
00:10:54,833 --> 00:10:57,000
What a bummer
that the padel tennis deal fell through.

164
00:10:57,083 --> 00:10:58,708
That was a sweet little deal.

165
00:10:59,250 --> 00:11:00,875
Remember what Jeff Bezos said, man.

166
00:11:00,958 --> 00:11:03,625
You always have to anticipate
a certain degree of failure.

167
00:11:03,708 --> 00:11:06,208
What a bummer. No wonder Kevin quit on me.

168
00:11:06,291 --> 00:11:07,791
What are you going to do?

169
00:11:07,875 --> 00:11:10,916
He put Wirpul in charge.
He says he's trustworthy.

170
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:13,208
What? Wait, why is he named
after a washing machine?

171
00:11:13,291 --> 00:11:15,000
Wirpul, dude. You know him.

172
00:11:15,708 --> 00:11:17,625
The one who cleans up your shit
when you go to the yacht.

173
00:11:17,708 --> 00:11:20,291
No, I don't go to the yacht
to hang out with your crew.

174
00:11:20,375 --> 00:11:22,166
And I'm always kind of wasted.

175
00:11:23,750 --> 00:11:24,625
Look. Him.

176
00:11:24,708 --> 00:11:26,416
Yeah, I know him. He's cool. I like him.

177
00:11:26,500 --> 00:11:28,291
-He's a badass, right?
-Yeah, totally.

178
00:11:28,375 --> 00:11:30,583
So what?
Is he gonna be the new captain or what?

179
00:11:31,583 --> 00:11:33,625
Look, we gotta give him a chance.

180
00:11:34,125 --> 00:11:35,666
Everyone deserves a break.

181
00:11:36,166 --> 00:11:38,041
He might even end up being my hero.

182
00:11:38,125 --> 00:11:39,416
You're a great guy, man.

183
00:11:40,625 --> 00:11:42,041
Yeah, I'm a fucking badass.

184
00:11:43,916 --> 00:11:46,666
19 HOURS BEFORE THE HURRICANE

185
00:11:48,291 --> 00:11:49,166
Mochis!

186
00:11:55,333 --> 00:11:56,416
Mom.

187
00:11:56,500 --> 00:11:58,250
-Honey.
-How did it go?

188
00:11:58,333 --> 00:12:00,666
-Good.
-You sold everything, huh?

189
00:12:00,750 --> 00:12:02,250
Mochis saved the day.

190
00:12:02,333 --> 00:12:03,333
Mochis is a hero.

191
00:12:08,541 --> 00:12:10,750
Why are you acting like such a fool?

192
00:12:12,083 --> 00:12:15,333
-Guess.
-What, did you win the lottery or what?

193
00:12:15,416 --> 00:12:16,291
Almost.

194
00:12:16,875 --> 00:12:18,000
Oh, just tell me.

195
00:12:18,708 --> 00:12:20,333
I got promoted on the ship.

196
00:12:21,166 --> 00:12:22,041
Captain!

197
00:12:22,125 --> 00:12:24,416
Captain starting tomorrow, Mom!

198
00:12:26,875 --> 00:12:28,583
You're going to be on watch.

199
00:12:29,375 --> 00:12:31,875
That damn Kevin always finds a way
to screw you over.

200
00:12:31,958 --> 00:12:34,041
So what? I've always wanted to be captain.

201
00:12:34,125 --> 00:12:36,541
But you're going to start during a storm?
Are you crazy?

202
00:12:36,625 --> 00:12:38,083
It's just a little storm.

203
00:12:38,166 --> 00:12:40,458
That's why
they're canceling school, Wirpul.

204
00:12:41,291 --> 00:12:43,791
Besides, you're going to get sick as hell.

205
00:12:44,291 --> 00:12:45,875
You always get sick during storms.

206
00:12:45,958 --> 00:12:48,166
I'm finally a boat's captain.

207
00:12:49,375 --> 00:12:54,041
It pays better. Neither of us has been
doing well lately and we need the money.

208
00:12:54,125 --> 00:12:55,333
Oh, Chayo.

209
00:12:57,500 --> 00:13:00,291
Wirpul's absolutely right, isn't he?

210
00:13:01,666 --> 00:13:02,958
You need the money.

211
00:13:03,041 --> 00:13:05,041
Especially now that you'll be homeless.

212
00:13:05,791 --> 00:13:06,875
Here we go again.

213
00:13:07,833 --> 00:13:09,750
With great sadness in my heart, Chayito.

214
00:13:09,833 --> 00:13:14,125
With great sadness in my heart, Carmen,
I'm telling you we're not leaving here.

215
00:13:14,208 --> 00:13:15,125
Oh, Chayito.

216
00:13:15,208 --> 00:13:17,000
Because this is our home too.

217
00:13:17,500 --> 00:13:21,125
And if you want to sell it,
you're going to have to buy my half first.

218
00:13:24,208 --> 00:13:28,208
If my husband let you guys live here,
it was out of sheer pity.

219
00:13:28,291 --> 00:13:29,708
But my husband isn't here anymore.

220
00:13:31,666 --> 00:13:34,625
I need you to vacate the house
by the end of the month.

221
00:13:42,958 --> 00:13:45,166
Ah, what's gotten into her, huh?

222
00:13:45,250 --> 00:13:46,791
All bark and no bite, Mom.

223
00:13:46,875 --> 00:13:49,083
But she's such a pain in the ass.

224
00:13:49,708 --> 00:13:51,583
Your dad left this house to us.

225
00:13:51,666 --> 00:13:53,125
What? He wasn't my dad.

226
00:13:53,208 --> 00:13:55,291
What do you mean he wasn't your dad?

227
00:13:55,375 --> 00:13:58,333
What, you think I had you
by immaculate conception?

228
00:13:58,416 --> 00:13:59,791
Well, he wasn't my dad.

229
00:14:00,791 --> 00:14:01,916
Just shut up.

230
00:14:02,625 --> 00:14:03,541
Mochis!

231
00:14:04,041 --> 00:14:05,708
Come here, Mochis. Come on.

232
00:14:14,291 --> 00:14:15,166
Hello?

233
00:14:15,250 --> 00:14:17,333
<i>-How are you, Wirpul?</i>
-Who is this?

234
00:14:17,416 --> 00:14:19,375
<i>It's Nico, dude. Your boss.</i>

235
00:14:20,458 --> 00:14:21,875
Mr. Nicolás. Sorry.

236
00:14:21,958 --> 00:14:23,291
You never call me.

237
00:14:23,375 --> 00:14:25,250
<i>You weren't the captain, but you are now.</i>

238
00:14:25,333 --> 00:14:26,791
I really appreciate the chance.

239
00:14:26,875 --> 00:14:29,666
<i>Kevin told me you're trustworthy.</i>
<i>You're a whiz with the boat.</i>

240
00:14:29,750 --> 00:14:31,958
<i>I saw on the news</i>
<i>that the storm's going to be bad.</i>

241
00:14:32,041 --> 00:14:33,875
<i>Take good care of my yacht.</i>

242
00:14:33,958 --> 00:14:34,916
Of course, boss.

243
00:14:35,500 --> 00:14:38,208
I'll tie it down tight on all sides.

244
00:14:38,291 --> 00:14:40,791
Don't worry. The storm's going to be calm.

245
00:14:40,875 --> 00:14:42,583
<i>That's right, man.</i>
<i>We've got to stay optimistic.</i>

246
00:14:42,666 --> 00:14:44,375
<i>Do you have a sailor yet?</i>

247
00:14:44,458 --> 00:14:47,166
A sailor? No, sir, but I'm working on it.

248
00:14:47,250 --> 00:14:49,083
<i>Call me if anything goes wrong, okay?</i>

249
00:14:49,166 --> 00:14:51,291
<i>I'll buy you two pounds of pastor,</i>
<i>and anything you want, a six-pack,</i>

250
00:14:51,375 --> 00:14:52,791
<i>whatever, so you can't say…</i>

251
00:14:52,875 --> 00:14:54,208
-Yes.
<i>-…I don't spoil you guys.</i>

252
00:14:54,291 --> 00:14:55,291
Yes, boss, thank you.

253
00:14:55,375 --> 00:14:57,375
<i>All right then, Wirpul,</i>
<i>take it easy, okay?</i>

254
00:14:58,458 --> 00:15:00,375
Couldn't you help me out with the door?

255
00:15:00,458 --> 00:15:01,958
You don't have a sailor.

256
00:15:11,416 --> 00:15:12,625
What about Marlon?

257
00:15:13,125 --> 00:15:14,750
He's busy on another boat.

258
00:15:15,250 --> 00:15:18,416
Everyone's busy or afraid of the storm.

259
00:15:23,125 --> 00:15:24,291
I have an idea.

260
00:15:26,125 --> 00:15:27,958
No, no fucking way. No, no, no.

261
00:15:28,041 --> 00:15:30,250
Why not? He's a good mechanic.

262
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:33,458
Jacking cars
doesn't make you a mechanic, Mom.

263
00:15:34,250 --> 00:15:37,500
I'd rather go down alone than ask
that asshole for anything. No way!

264
00:15:37,583 --> 00:15:38,875
What do you mean, "go down alone"?

265
00:15:38,958 --> 00:15:40,375
It's just a figure of speech.

266
00:15:41,291 --> 00:15:42,958
Besides, he's afraid of the sea.

267
00:15:44,125 --> 00:15:46,791
Look, if the boat breaks down
he can fix it.

268
00:15:47,541 --> 00:15:51,333
Besides, whether you like it or not,
Candi's your brother and you need him.

269
00:15:52,625 --> 00:15:55,875
Candi is not my brother,
and I don't need him.

270
00:15:55,958 --> 00:15:59,000
No, that bastard's just gonna get me
into trouble. No, no.

271
00:16:05,125 --> 00:16:06,750
-I'm here.
-Hey!

272
00:16:07,250 --> 00:16:08,250
Look.

273
00:16:09,291 --> 00:16:10,291
What's that?

274
00:16:11,500 --> 00:16:13,166
I sold the gun to Mr. Rafa.

275
00:16:14,041 --> 00:16:15,000
Did it work?

276
00:16:50,291 --> 00:16:51,500
Give me one, then.

277
00:16:58,291 --> 00:17:00,166
Not even a fly showed up, Candi.

278
00:17:01,708 --> 00:17:04,541
I wouldn't stick around if they were going
to leave my hair and face like that.

279
00:17:04,625 --> 00:17:05,750
I'm serious.

280
00:17:07,791 --> 00:17:10,041
-What are you looking for, anyway?
-No smokes?

281
00:17:10,125 --> 00:17:12,166
They're right there,
behind the nail polish.

282
00:17:18,541 --> 00:17:20,291
You're always scolding them.

283
00:17:20,375 --> 00:17:21,750
They're scared of you.

284
00:17:21,833 --> 00:17:23,750
You're the one
who should be afraid of me, you bastard.

285
00:17:23,833 --> 00:17:25,708
You don't even
respect your own mother!

286
00:17:32,458 --> 00:17:33,833
What are you looking for?

287
00:17:34,916 --> 00:17:37,500
Can't I smoke a cigarette
outside my house?

288
00:17:37,583 --> 00:17:39,791
What the fuck?
You're on our side, aren't you?

289
00:17:40,416 --> 00:17:42,791
-Weren't you guys leaving already?
-Where to?

290
00:17:42,875 --> 00:17:44,666
Oh, that, I don't know.

291
00:17:44,750 --> 00:17:47,166
You seem pretty calm, don't you?
Go pack your bags.

292
00:17:47,250 --> 00:17:49,125
-What suitcases, Candi?
-Don't you have any?

293
00:17:49,208 --> 00:17:51,833
-Candi! Candi.
-You're sick. You're sick, dude!

294
00:17:51,916 --> 00:17:54,041
Wirpul has to watch over the yacht.

295
00:17:57,000 --> 00:17:59,666
So what, you want my blessing?
Come here, son.

296
00:17:59,750 --> 00:18:02,208
-In the name of the Father…
-They made him captain.

297
00:18:03,500 --> 00:18:04,458
Captain!

298
00:18:04,541 --> 00:18:06,291
And he doesn't have a sailor.

299
00:18:06,375 --> 00:18:09,375
It's dangerous for him
to be alone in the upcoming storm.

300
00:18:09,458 --> 00:18:11,666
My mom thinks that stealing car parts
makes you a mechanic.

301
00:18:11,750 --> 00:18:14,125
Asshole. And who made you captain
if you're just the maid?

302
00:18:14,208 --> 00:18:15,791
-Your mom's the maid.
-My mom?

303
00:18:15,875 --> 00:18:17,666
-Your mom's the maid!
-It's called doing someone's laundry!

304
00:18:17,750 --> 00:18:20,041
I'm the captain because I've got
a ton of experience as a sailor.

305
00:18:21,458 --> 00:18:24,458
Please, Candi, Wirpul needs you.

306
00:18:28,916 --> 00:18:30,333
Well, it doesn't look like it.

307
00:18:32,625 --> 00:18:35,000
You just want people to beg you,
don't you, asshole?

308
00:18:36,500 --> 00:18:38,666
Aren't you the one
looking for money everywhere?

309
